(* #FORMAT FormatThese *)

=> First documents

This chapter walks you from an empty file to your first PDF.  It
assumes you have installed Patoline from opam (`opam install patoline`)
and have `patoline` on your $PATH.  If that is not the case, see the
top-level `README.md` for installation instructions.

=> Paragraphs and sections

While it is always possible to compile empty documents with Patoline,
these may not be the most interesting ones.  Patoline does not have any
real graphical interface for now, although it includes a quite good
emacs mode, partly generated automatically to match the grammar you
select.  The instructions are given in chapter \sectref("Editors").
So let us assume that you managed to install patoline on your system
and have opened a text editor on a blank file.  You would then write
your first Patoline document \verb{hello.txp}:

###
Hello, world !
###

To see the result, just write in a terminal the following command,
in the same directory as your file:

###
patoline --driver Pdf --format FormatArticle -o hello.pdf hello.txp
###

The result should be a PDF file named \verb{hello.pdf}, with one
page containing your text.  The choice of PDF files as the default
output format is only to avoid breaking the habits of TeX users.
Patoline can output its result to other formats, and new output
drivers can be written quite easily, as we will see in section
\sectref("Writing output drivers").

There is also a way to typeset several paragraphs: by writing them
with at least one line of blank space between them, like on the
following example:

###
This is my first paragraph.

This is my second one.
###

Both paragraphs are typeset in the same font, separated by an
appropriate vertical glue that depends on the format.

=> Headings

To structure a document, you can introduce headings.  The default
way to do so is:

###
=> Introduction

Welcome to Patoline.
###

The [=>] symbol denotes a section.  Subsections are introduced by
[==>], and so on.  The format picks the font size and spacing for
each level; you do not need to tune them yourself.

=> Maths

Patoline has inline maths written between [$$] markers and display
maths written between [$$$] markers.  For instance:

###
Let $f : x \mapsto x^2$.  Then

$$$f(x + y) = x^2 + 2xy + y^2.$$
###

The maths implementation accepts a subset of LaTeX syntax.  See the
[PatoMaths] chapter for a full reference.

=> Verbatim

For code listings, use [###] ... [###]:

###
let rec factorial n =
  if n <= 1 then 1 else n * factorial (n - 1)
###

Verbatim blocks preserve whitespace, use a monospaced font, and are
not wrapped.

=> Common pitfalls

  - **Missing blank line between paragraphs.**  Without an empty
    line between two paragraphs, Patoline concatenates them on the
    same line.

  - **Tab characters.**  Patoline is sensitive to indentation.  Use
    spaces only.

  - **A `.txp` file without a final newline.**  The parser sometimes
    refuses to consume the last paragraph if the file does not end
    with a newline.

